{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Bill of Rights including social rights to health, housing, education and decent work; adopt EU Social Chapter and introduce a minimum wage; increase child benefit and restore benefits for 16–17s; pensioner package and ending means-testing for residential care; NHS investment (+£35m p.a.), end internal market, ban tobacco advertising; free nursery education for all 3–4-year-olds, restore student grants, hire 700 teachers; build 20,000 affordable homes and renovate substandard stock; proportional representation and vote at 16. TEXT_F: Oppose EU Social Chapter and a national minimum wage; prioritize tax cuts, deregulation, and spending restraint; selective schooling by aptitude and support for grammar schools; tougher discipline, inspections, and testing; more police, CCTV expansion, mandatory minimum sentences; oppose PR, a new Bill of Rights, and devolution while upholding the Union; NHS real-terms increases and respite care for carers; welfare-to-work expansion and anti-fraud measures.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile weights care and equality most, which are strongly and repeatedly invoked by TEXT_C through social rights, redistribution, public service expansion, minimum wage adoption, and universal early-years provision. TEXT_F leans more on authority, loyalty, and proportionality (law-and-order, Unionism, merit/market mechanisms) and explicitly rejects several equality-focused measures, offering fewer and more limited care-oriented commitments, so it fits the profile less well.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
